#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166569 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166952 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3cb2fcb-3457-5a89-8968-6c0f2c2cfa83" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Product of Cosines"}] :question/id #uuid "177a0110-424b-5ac3-a24e-e817cbcf479c" :question/math-academy-id "e-21823" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{\\pi/4}^{\\pi/2} \\cos(9x)\\cos(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these equations, we get the following product-to-sum identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9x-x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9x+x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)+\\frac{1}{20}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10x)]}_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi ))+\\frac{1}{20}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}(0-0)+\\frac{1}{20}(0-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166570 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166953 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a371de4-7c3f-5687-bd3a-d3ab5ab803ce" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Product of Sines"}] :question/id #uuid "ebf57ac7-29e3-595e-8a72-032e10c470aa" :question/math-academy-id "e-21824" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int \\sin(12x)\\sin(5x)\\,\\text{d}x .$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtracting the second equation from the first, we get the following product-to-sum identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12x-5x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12x+5x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(17x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(12x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(17x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{14}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)-\\frac{1}{34}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(17x)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166571 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166954 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d3b7f35-fcd1-5443-b0ea-0b06e016e0c3" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Product of Sine and Cosine"}] :question/id #uuid "14466847-40e1-5a4f-b50a-23b62590f0a2" :question/math-academy-id "e-21825" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\pi/2} \\sin(2x)\\cos(4x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for sine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these equations, we get the following product-to-sum identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+4x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-4x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-2x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)+\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))+\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}(-1-1)+\\frac{1}{4}(-1-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167279 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166953 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a371de4-7c3f-5687-bd3a-d3ab5ab803ce" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Product of Sines"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "7ed943c4-5d0d-5d06-8135-a0d7948bb9ae" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164892}] :question/id #uuid "7721acbc-048b-50e9-95a0-46bf90727bd9" :question/math-academy-id "q-340386" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral.$\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π/2}\\sin (3x)\\sin (6x)dx =$ {{field-1}}"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167281 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166954 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d3b7f35-fcd1-5443-b0ea-0b06e016e0c3" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Product of Sine and Cosine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "ad646b86-c5af-5b0e-9b9f-e5764cd74edd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164899}] :question/id #uuid "3d707fb1-cb96-50fe-b7df-5abab3385ab6" :question/math-academy-id "q-340390" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral.$\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π/2}\\sin (3x)\\cos (6x)dx =$ {{field-1}}"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314946 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166952 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3cb2fcb-3457-5a89-8968-6c0f2c2cfa83" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Product of Cosines"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7511559d-1260-514d-8be4-3e7cc538c779" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)+\\frac{1}{32}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(16x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312908} {:answer/id #uuid "9f1be971-8240-5314-bbe0-c73114377645" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)+\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(16x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313227} {:answer/id #uuid "c3edf8c4-f1ba-521f-a323-8a7f41301969" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)+\\frac{1}{11}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(11x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313513} {:answer/id #uuid "d7c77b1d-3b97-5c81-b48d-a10543aa60d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)-\\frac{1}{32}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(16x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313675} {:answer/id #uuid "e72f93f6-ae01-5923-a6b6-ede474ef1bb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)-\\frac{1}{32}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(16x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313808}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7511559d-1260-514d-8be4-3e7cc538c779" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)+\\frac{1}{32}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(16x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312908} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe0bde4e-5f8d-5d47-a561-5a92587b89b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314692}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b947f05f-96e9-547a-a382-8c4c319c6eca" :question/math-academy-id "q-340455" :question/problem "$\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(11x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these equations, we get the following product-to-sum identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(11x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(11x-5x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(11x+5x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(16x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(11x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(16x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)+\\frac{1}{32}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(16x)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377336 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166953 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a371de4-7c3f-5687-bd3a-d3ab5ab803ce" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Product of Sines"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "afe8e06b-8a1b-5efd-8e1b-855c951f5a55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377338}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "afe8e06b-8a1b-5efd-8e1b-855c951f5a55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377338} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd5b9f84-42a3-57ce-9ab1-dbc551ef01e8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377337}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dc5d8ff1-9935-5447-83ed-23682e5487b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-340476" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n${\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtracting the second equation from the first, we get the following product-to-sum identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x-3x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x+3x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)]}_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))-\\frac{1}{16}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(0-1)-\\frac{1}{16}(0-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314960 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166954 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d3b7f35-fcd1-5443-b0ea-0b06e016e0c3" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Product of Sine and Cosine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f9486d2b-7ce0-5f6e-a396-c474a1abd86a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313970}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9486d2b-7ce0-5f6e-a396-c474a1abd86a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313970} :answer-field/id #uuid "56c732ec-4884-5281-a12b-632144414c6f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314256}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c6c293fa-013e-59cd-b66e-3459611554eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-340489" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for sine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these equations, we get the following product-to-sum identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9x+3x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9x-3x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{24}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12x)-\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{24}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))-\\frac{1}{12}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{24}(1-1)-\\frac{1}{12}(-1-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314961 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166954 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d3b7f35-fcd1-5443-b0ea-0b06e016e0c3" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Product of Sine and Cosine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ddeaca9e-e824-533c-bc58-3ec2b3a38f8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313720}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ddeaca9e-e824-533c-bc58-3ec2b3a38f8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313720} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce118ddd-47bd-5cab-bad9-6d3152c9b1db" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314578}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d43d9ab4-a760-5c76-927f-c8696da3c953" :question/math-academy-id "q-340491" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n${\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for sine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these equations, we get the following product-to-sum identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9x+x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9x-x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{20}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10x)-\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)]}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{20}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\pi ))-\\frac{1}{16}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\pi )) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{20}(1-(-1))-\\frac{1}{16}(1-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314962 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166954 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d3b7f35-fcd1-5443-b0ea-0b06e016e0c3" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Product of Sine and Cosine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d074817c-9881-518c-b1ea-75ba1846c23c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313605}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d074817c-9881-518c-b1ea-75ba1846c23c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313605} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae1a4a9f-47b6-58b2-8ec5-aab72b4b9f7d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314478}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "11434a0f-ad34-5cb2-96ed-81af7783f37f" :question/math-academy-id "q-340492" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for sine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these equations, we get the following product-to-sum identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x+x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x-x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)-\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)]}_{0}^{3\\pi /4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{16}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))-\\frac{1}{12}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{9\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{16}(1-1)-\\frac{1}{12}(0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
